The rehabilitation and development of the Great Zimbabwe World Heritage Site project aims to promote the tourism and cultural heritage of Zimbabwe by enhancing the WHS and strengthening national capacities in terms of heritage enhancement and development of related economic activities. 
The specific objectives of the project are to enhance the value of the Great Zimbabwe site, strengthen the site's cultural and educational programming, diversify the associated tourism offerings, and attract a wider audience, thereby providing multiple benefits in terms of achieving the SDGs.
1.	Capacity building of the National Museums and Monuments of Zimbabwe (NMMZ) under the Ministry of Home Affairs and Cultural Heritage for conservation and documentation of the site;
2.	Renovation and upgrading of the infrastructure with a new interpretive system and new visitor facilities;
3.	Delegation of a private operator to conduct commercial management and marketing development of the World Heritage site,
4.	Development of a rural land management plan and support to local communities for the implementation of a cultural program within a cultural hub and to improve community-based management of natural resources and risks.

The Joint Devolution Programme (JDP) is a 4-year initiative led by UNDP, UNICEF, and UN Women, building on their previous support to devolution to enhance governance and service delivery structures in 8 target counties focusing on six key areas: 
1. National–county coordination (e.g., interactions with key national institutions like Treasury, COG, CRA,
2. County political systems (e.g., Governors, MCAs, CECs);
3.County administrative systems (e.g., CPSB, Treasury, departments);
4. Ward-level structures (e.g., Ward Committees);
5. Facility-level operations (e.g., health centres, ECDs, water groups);
6.Public participation and accountability (e.g., citizens, CSOs, local businesses).

This high-level conference on Sustainable Ocean Economy and Climate Change Adaptation organized by the Governments of Viet Nam and Norway and UNDP in hybrid format in Hanoi, Viet Nam brings together leaders, practitioners, scientists, experts, private sector and civil society together  to: 
1. Discuss the key opportunities to promote sustainable utilization of ocean resources as well as key challenges posed by the Covid-19 crisis, climate change and environmental pollution including the Global Agreement on Plastic Pollution Ocean; 
2. Identify opportunities to accelerate actions to protect ocean ecosystems for economic development and enhance the adaptation and resilience of vulnerable countries and communities;
3. Share experiences, best practices and research results, to enhance knowledge of successful sustainable ocean economy, climate change adaption strategies and actions; and
4. Encourage networking of South-South and South-North collaboration, foster cooperation and develop synergies between initiatives. 
Key Conference results and recommended priority actions will be set out in a Co-Chairs' Joint Statement.

